1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:07,300
Meet Riley, a customer of Juniper Goods. The assistant helps with a familiar
service request.

2
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:14,800
This edited recording follows the customer’s request into the agent’s
actions and the evidence Rook checks.

3
00:00:15,300 --> 00:00:22,300
The Returns & Refunds Assistant connects the conversation to business tools.
Each tool has a specific responsibility.

4
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:29,800
The outcome to verify is the refund receipt. A convincing answer alone does
not establish that the action happened.

5
00:00:30,300 --> 00:00:37,300
For developers, start with the agent source and requirements. Follow a
failed criterion into the tool that owns the business rule.

6
00:00:37,800 --> 00:00:44,800
Both audiences can connect the customer’s expectations to the same
observable business outcome.

7
00:00:45,300 --> 00:00:52,300
Start with the normal request: “Refund ORD-100 for me.”

8
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:59,800
Inspect the reply alongside the refund receipt. This application is
connected to the configured language model.

9
00:01:00,300 --> 00:01:07,300
Read ORD-100, then refund the same order in the next turn. Check that the
receipt matches Riley’s order and appears only once.

10
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:14,800
The conversation identifier stays the same across these turns. Starting a
new session gives the next scenario separate business state.

11
00:01:15,300 --> 00:01:22,300
Now challenge the business rule: Refund ORD-100 while the payment provider
is unavailable.

12
00:01:22,800 --> 00:01:29,800
A failed refund must not be reported as a completed payment. Compare what
the assistant says with the ledger and tool trace.

13
00:01:30,300 --> 00:01:37,300
Choose After the fix and repeat the request in a fresh session. This browser
selection controls its own conversation.

14
00:01:37,800 --> 00:01:44,800
The Rook run selects its version separately. Correlate evidence by
conversation and version before comparing outcomes.

15
00:01:45,300 --> 00:01:52,300
Open this edition in Rook’s hosted web UI. Review the agent, its business
functions and the scenario collection.

16
00:01:52,800 --> 00:01:59,800
The collection contains 18 authored categories. These are reviewable
examples; they are not a claim that all 18 tests have passed.

17
00:02:00,300 --> 00:02:07,300
Open the selected scenario and read its acceptance criteria before looking
at the result.

18
00:02:07,800 --> 00:02:14,800
Check the customer goal, expected business behavior, selected connection
profile and evidence required to decide the case.

19
00:02:15,300 --> 00:02:22,300
The original Rook run failed: the agent claimed a successful refund even
though the payment failure left no refund receipt.

20
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:29,800
This is a recorded, focused run. The report’s scope matters as much as the
verdict.

21
00:02:30,300 --> 00:02:37,300
Open the evidence for that exact conversation. Follow the request into the
model reply and tool arguments.

22
00:02:37,800 --> 00:02:44,800
Then inspect the refund receipt. A denied attempt is different from a
completed business write.

23
00:02:45,300 --> 00:02:52,300
The updated run passed this check. It reported the payment failure and left
the refund ledger unchanged.

24
00:02:52,800 --> 00:02:59,800
Compare the criteria and evidence across versions. Keep any remaining
failures or missing observations visible.

25
00:03:00,300 --> 00:03:07,300
The role-play scenario challenges whether the agent will abandon its
business rule when the user changes the framing.

26
00:03:07,800 --> 00:03:14,800
Red-teaming also covers instructions hidden in retrieved content, private-
data exposure, external exports and policy bypass.

27
00:03:15,300 --> 00:03:22,300
The MCP profile invokes the agent through MCP and keeps the multi-turn
conversation together.

28
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:29,800
A separate read-only verifier inspects the same conversation’s business
evidence. It does not repeat the action to check whether it happened.

29
00:03:30,300 --> 00:03:37,300
Add reliability, slow-dependency and response-quality checks when they
matter to this journey.

30
00:03:37,800 --> 00:03:44,800
Cost checks require complete provider usage. Missing measurements remain
unverified; illustrative budgets are not product guarantees.

31
00:03:45,300 --> 00:03:52,300
Close with Riley’s original need and the business rule the audience can
recognize.

32
00:03:52,800 --> 00:03:59,800
Use the demo folder’s setup and interactive Rook guide to repeat the check.
Preserve the real result, including Fail or Unable to Verify.
